The request targets lateral hillslope-to-stream travel times, flow convergence along topographic flowpaths, and column-to-column groundwater exchange. Every one of these mechanisms is lateral and inter-column. ELM in this framework is single-column, 1-D, with NO lateral transport, no routing, no column-to-column exchange, and no regional water table. The PRIMARY mechanism the user asks about is exactly what the stack cannot represent.

I will therefore return an infeasible/partial verdict. The framework can do a proxy: characterize the vertical water balance and drainage flux (QDRAI) at hillslope positions along a representative topographic transect (ridge → midslope → valley), which supplies the recharge/drainage boundary condition that a coupled lateral-flow model (PFLOTRAN, or a routing/hillslope-storage model) would need. But travel times and convergence themselves require a coupler that is not built. I flag this honestly and enumerate a small transect sampling design only as the vertical-flux precursor, not as an answer to travel time.

Because the brief lists no observations, validation is limited to physical-expectation checks. I keep N small: a single representative hillslope transect of ~4 positions (ridge, upper slope, footslope, valley/riparian) with WTD-informed warm start and spin-up, since drainage flux at valley bottoms depends on initialized storage.

